'''Jōkaku Toshi''' (城郭都市, *Jōkaku Toshi*) refers to {{ill|fortified cities|en|Fortified city|12=draft}} that are enclosed and protected by {{ill|city walls|en|City wall|12=draft}}<ref>Also referred to as '''城壁都市''' (*Jōheki Toshi*) or '''囲郭都市''' (*Ikaku Toshi*). In French: {{ill|ville fortifiée|fr|Ville fortifiée|12=draft}}, Cité fortifiée, or ville avec rempart; in English: walled city or fortified city; in German: befestigte Stadt.</ref><ref>Sometimes called '''城塞都市''' (*Jōsai Toshi*), though in this case, '''{{ill|citadel|en|Citadel|12=draft}}''' does not imply its literal English meaning.</ref>. Defensive structures such as {{ill|earthworks|en|Earthworks (engineering)|12=draft}} and {{ill|moats|en|Moat|12=draft}} were also utilized.

== Overview ==
The history of fortified cities dates back to the {{ill|ancient civilizations|en|Ancient history|12=draft}} of {{ill|Egypt|en|Ancient Egypt|12=draft}}, {{ill|Mesopotamia|en|Mesopotamia|12=draft}}, {{ill|Indus Valley|en|Indus Valley civilization|12=draft}}, {{ill|China|en|Ancient China|12=draft}}, and {{ill|ancient Greece|en|Ancient Greece|12=draft}}.

The origins of fortified cities can be traced to {{ill|moated settlements|en|Moated site|12=draft}}. These settlements emerged during the {{ill|Neolithic period|en|Neolithic|12=draft}} with the advent of agriculture and were universally observed in many parts of the world. As wealth accumulated in agricultural settlements, they became targets for external threats. Early defensive measures included digging moats and building earthen mounds. These defenses evolved, with moats deepened and filled with water, and the construction of higher walls made of {{ill|rammed earth|en|Rammed earth|12=draft}}, {{ill|bricks|en|Brick|12=draft}}, or {{ill|stone|en|Stone|12=draft}}. Fortified cities developed primarily in regions where urban civilizations thrived, such as the {{ill|Middle East|en|Middle East|12=draft}}, {{ill|Europe|en|Europe|12=draft}}, and {{ill|China|en|China|12=draft}}, where frequent conflicts occurred between tribes and nations.

However, the high population density within fortified cities often led to poor sanitation and the rapid spread of diseases, especially in low-lying areas.

=== Europe ===
