Most major cities in continental Europe before the medieval period were surrounded by high walls. The capital of the {{ill|Byzantine Empire|en|Byzantine Empire|12=draft}}, {{ill|Constantinople|en|Constantinople|12=draft}}, was fortified with three layers of walls over 10 meters high. Likewise, the central city of the {{ill|Islamic Golden Age|en|Islamic Golden Age|12=draft}}, {{ill|Baghdad|en|Baghdad|12=draft}}, was surrounded by circular walls with seven gates and a diameter of 2.35 kilometers.

Fortified cities typically featured {{ill|city gates|en|City gate|12=draft}}, moats, and {{ill|drawbridges|en|Drawbridge|12=draft}}. Some cities added multiple layers of walls, with {{ill|watchtowers|en|Watchtower|12=draft}} and {{ill|embrasures|en|Embrasure|12=draft}} for defense. Within the walls, fortifications often included {{ill|citadels|en|Citadel|12=draft}} where {{ill|nobles|en|Nobility|12=draft}} resided, along with agricultural land and wells to sustain prolonged sieges.

=== Asia ===
In {{ill|China|en|Ancient China|12=draft}}, the original meaning of "城" (*cheng*, city) referred to the defensive walls that surrounded settlements. Later, it came to encompass the entire enclosed area. Chinese city walls often included {{ill|gatehouses|en|Gatehouse|12=draft}} and multi-layered fortifications known as {{ill|Oucheng|ja|甕城|12=draft}}, which protected the gates. Examples of well-preserved city walls include those in {{ill|Nanjing|en|Nanjing|12=draft}} and {{ill|Xi'an|en|Xi'an|12=draft}}.

In {{ill|Japan|en|Japan|12=draft}}, early fortified settlements like {{ill|Yoshinogari|en|Yoshinogari|12=draft}} during the {{ill|Yayoi period|en|Yayoi period|12=draft}} featured moats and earthen walls, but these disappeared as centralized {{ill|Ritsuryō|en|Ritsuryō|12=draft}} states developed. Later periods saw the emergence of {{ill|castle towns|en|Castle town|12=draft}} like {{ill|Kamakura|en|Kamakura|12=draft}} and {{ill|Edo|en|Edo|12=draft}}, which were surrounded by moats and earthen walls.

=== Africa ===
In Africa, notable examples include {{ill|Buhen|en|Buhen|12=draft}} in ancient Egypt and {{ill|Great Zimbabwe|en|Great Zimbabwe|12=draft}}.

